[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 821,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 764,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125213,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 84,
        "headingId": 764,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Congenital melanocytic naevus (CMN) is a benign melanocytic neoplasm present at birth or appearing in the first year of life (late or tardive congenital naevus). Blue naevi can also arise at birth but are discussed in a separate section.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:12:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 822,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 765,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125214,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 84,
        "headingId": 765,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8761/0 Congenital melanocytic naevus</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:12:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 823,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 766,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125215,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 84,
        "headingId": 766,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2F20.2Z Congenital melanocytic naevus, unspecified</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:12:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 824,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 767,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125216,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 84,
        "headingId": 767,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> congenital naevocytic naevus; congenital naevus syndrome; neurocutaneous melanosis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:12:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 825,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 768,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125217,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 84,
        "headingId": 768,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:12:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 826,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 769,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125218,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 84,
        "headingId": 769,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>CMN can occur anywhere on the body {15692463}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:12:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 827,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 770,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125219,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 84,
        "headingId": 770,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>In adults, CMNs are categorized as small (&lt;&nbsp;15&nbsp;mm), intermediate (15&ndash;200&nbsp;mm), or giant (&gt;&nbsp;200&nbsp;mm). Small and intermediate CMNs typically present as symmetrical, oval or round, brown to black, well-circumscribed macules or plaques that are homogeneous in texture and often have prominent terminal hairs or a rugose or pebbly surface. Giant naevi may exhibit thickening of the skin and soft tissue, and those affecting the scalp and back can involve the CNS (neurocutaneous melanosis) {22980258}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:12:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 828,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 771,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125220,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 84,
        "headingId": 771,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>About 1% of newborns have a small CMN. Giant congenital naevi affect 5&nbsp;newborns per 100&nbsp;000&nbsp;births.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:12:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 829,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 772,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125221,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 84,
        "headingId": 772,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>CMN is a clonal proliferation of melanocytes caused by a postzygotic mutation.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:12:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 830,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 773,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125222,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 84,
        "headingId": 773,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The majority (~90%) of large CMNs harbour <em>NRAS</em> mutations {16888631}. <em>BRAF</em> p.V600E mutations are less common (5&ndash;10%) {25490715}. <em>KRAS</em>, <em>APC</em>, and <em>MET</em> mutations and <em>ZEB2</em>::<em>ALK</em> and <em>SOX5</em>::<em>RAF1</em> gene fusions have also been observed {30359577}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:12:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 837,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 780,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125223,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 84,
        "headingId": 780,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>See <em>Clinical features</em>, above.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:12:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 831,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 774,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125224,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 84,
        "headingId": 774,
        "headingText": "<p>CMNs are larger than common acquired naevi and frequently involve the reticular dermis and subcutis, with splaying of naevus cells between collagen bundles and around dermal appendages and neurovascular bundles {33464753; 20442637; 22771898}. In infants, there may be striking pagetoid scatter and confluence of the intraepidermal nests in the centre of the lesions. Cells in the dermis often change from large epithelioid type&nbsp;A naevus cells in the upper dermis to small lymphocyte-like type&nbsp;B&nbsp;cells to spindled type&nbsp;C cells in the deep reticular dermis. There is no significant atypia or mitotic activity {28078671}.</p>\r\n<h5>Immunohistochemistry</h5>\r\n<p>CMNs usually express the melanocytic markers melan-A and SOX10 and show a gradual decrease of HMB45 expression with depth.</p>\r\n<h5>Differential diagnosis</h5>\r\n<p>CMN with an atypical junctional proliferation of melanocytes may simulate melanoma in&nbsp;situ, but such areas are typically restricted to the centre of the lesion. Cases with increased cellularity can be difficult to distinguish from naevoid melanoma. Deep, atypical, or clustered mitotic figures, nuclear enlargement, pleomorphism, hyperchromatism, and sheet-like growth {26602414; 22176022; 15370108} are among the most important features pointing towards melanoma.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:12:54"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 832,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 775,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125225,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 84,
        "headingId": 775,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:12:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 833,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 776,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125226,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 84,
        "headingId": 776,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Molecular analyses are usually unnecessary for the diagnosis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:12:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 836,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 779,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125227,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 84,
        "headingId": 779,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> large melanocytic naevi, usually present at birth or in early life; deep reticular dermal involvement, splaying of collagen, and perivascular and periadnexal distribution of naevus cells.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:12:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 834,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 777,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125228,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 84,
        "headingId": 777,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:12:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 835,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 778,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 125229,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 84,
        "headingId": 778,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The lifetime risk of melanoma arising in a CMN of any size is approximately 1&ndash;2%; for large CMNs with satellite naevi or CNS involvement it is as high as 10&ndash;15% {29999207; 28078671}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T17:12:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]